	------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	To activate the conda environment and start the jupyter notebook: 
	$	conda env create -f environment.yml
	$	source activate housecleaning_ac
	(housecleaning_ac) $ jupyter-lab

	To deactivate the conda environment
	$ source deactivate

	------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	This folder contains pre-processed data and post-processing routines used 
	for the analysis of reversal dynamics. The idea being to ensure the 
	reproductibility of the results contained in the article in preparation 
	"Conditional statistics of the large-scale reversal dynamics in square 
	Rayleigh–Bénard cells". For simplicity, the routines are divided in sections
	following the structure of the article. 

	- section03_identification_of_the_two_regimes.ipynb
	- section04_comparison_of_spatial_structures.ipynb
	- section05_comparison_of_temporal_evolution.ipynb
	- section06_cluster_analysis_cr_regime.ipynb
	- section07_cluster_analysis_ec_regime.ipynb
	- section08_evolution_of_dynamical_regimes.ipynb

	In addition to the appendices

	- section98_choice_of_cluster_number_cr.ipynb
	- section99_choice_of_cluster_number_ec.ipynb
	
	All of the figures are stored inside figures_for_article/

	------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
	Data is organised as to ensure the portability of the results. Only the 
	pre-processed data directly connected to the contents of the article is 
	contained here. The raw results from DNS being in compressed files. 

	Results were obtained in two phases. A first batch of numerical simulations 	
	was used to produce a set of POD modes and perform a preliminary analysis. 
	Modes were obtained using a Fortran routine included inside 
	HouseCleaning_AC/00_general_post/pod_using_fortran and results from this 
	part are contained inside data_from_pod/ which includes the eigenvalues 
	and the spatial eigenfunctions. 

	In the second batch of numerical simulations we use the POD modes from 
	the first batch to obtain modal amplitudes with a good temporal resolution 
	without storing the full fields. Results from these simulations are either 
	statistical fields and time-series of different global quantities. For this 
	part we only used the time-series. 

	Three types of time-series were produced
	
	- nusselt_${Ra}_${Pr}_${N}_${Set}.asc : [1]t 	[2]nuvol 	[3]nutop 	[4]nubot 	[5]nuviscous 	[6]nuthermal 	[7]nuleft 	[8]nuright 
	
	- global_${Ra}_${Pr}_${N}_${Set}.asc	: [1]t 	[2]l2d 		[3]ekin 	[4]enst 	[5]circul 		[6]phi_y 			[7]epot     [8]bpot 
																								[9]phi_b2 [10]phi_d [11]etemp [12]phi_b3 		[13]m2d 			[14]ia2d 		[15]ib2d,      
																								[16]mc2d 	[17]ic2d 	[18]dist 	[19]id2d

	-	modal_${Ra}_${Pr}_${N}_${Set}.asc		: [1]t 	[2]mode1 	[3]mode2 	[4]mode3 	[5]mode4 	[6]mode5 	[7]mode6
																								[8]mode1 	[9]mode2 	[10]mode3 [11]mode4 [12]mode5 [13]mode6
																								[14]mode1 [15]mode2 [16]mode3 [17]mode4 [18]mode5 [19]mode6	
	
	which correspond to the Nusselt numbers, the energy budget and the decomposition
	of the global angular impulse (see Castillo, Sergent & Rossi, 2016) and the 
	projections of the six most energetic modes into the temperature and velocity 
	fields, hence the 18 columns. 

	Because of the large number of records and the sampling frequency, the time series 
	are quite large. For simplicity, we pre-preprocessing the original files which were written
	as plain text into a hdf5 file format which supports compression using the following 
	piece of code in python 3. 

	>>> import pandas as pd
	>>> import numpy as np
	>>>
	>>> nus = pd.read_csv('./nusselt_200000000_4.3_1024_018to033.asc', sep='\\s+', header=0, 
													usecols=[0,1,2,3,4,5], names=['t','vol','top','bot','epsu','epst'])
	>>>
	>>> nus.to_hdf('./nusselt_200000000_4.3_1024_018to033.h5', '/nus', table=True, 
									mode='w', compression='blosc:lz4', complevel=9, fletcher32=True)
	>>>
	>>> glb = pd.read_csv('./global_50000000_3_512_016to186.asc', sep='\\s+', header=0, 
													usecols=[0,1,2,5,6,7,9,10,12,13,14], names=['t','l2d','ekin','phiy','epot','bpot','phid','etmp','m2d','ia2d','ib2d'])
	>>>
	>>> glb.to_hdf('./global_50000000_3_512_016to186.h5', '/glb', table=True, 
								mode='w', compression='blosc:lz4', complevel=9, fletcher32=True)

	>>> moda = pd.read_csv('./modal_50000000_4.3_512_060to259.asc', sep='\\s+',header=0,
		                 names=['t',
		                        'k1a','k2a','k3a','k4a','k5a','k6a',
		                        'k1b','k2b','k3b','k4b','k5b','k6b',
		                        'k1c','k2c','k3c','k4c','k5c','k6c'])
	>>>
	>>> for k in ['k1','k2','k3','k4','k5','k6']:
		  	moda[k]=-moda[k+'a']+moda[k+'b']-moda[k+'c']
		  	modb = moda.drop([k+'a',k+'b',k+'c'], axis=1)
	>>>
	>>> modb.to_hdf('./modal_50000000_4.3_512_060to259.h5', '/modb', table=True, 
									mode='w', compression='blosc:lz4', complevel=9, fletcher32=True)

